When a document repopulated for its session history entry creates a
child navigable, Ladybird restores the nested history the entry kept,
and traverses the child to it at once. So Ladybird now stops the lazy
loading of the child’s iframe there, and the restored document stays.

Blink and Gecko don’t meet this case: Both restore a new child
frame’s entry only in place of the frame’s own first load, and
only while the parent’s history load is in progress. In Blink,
NavigationControllerImpl::StartHistoryNavigationInNewSubframe() loads
the restored entry in place of a child frame’s first navigation, as
long as LocalFrameClientImpl::BeginNavigation() found the parent’s
IsBackForwardNavigationInProgress() true. In Gecko, nsDocShell::
MaybeHandleSubframeHistory() swaps the restored entry in while the
parent docshell IsLoadingFromSessionHistory(). So in both, a lazy frame
stays lazy — and once its lazy load starts after the parent has loaded,
it loads its src rather than the restored entry.

Both also stop a frame’s lazy loading when another load starts in
the frame or commits in it: Blink’s FrameLoader::StartNavigation()
and FrameLoader::CommitNavigation() call CancelPendingLazyLoad(), as
do LocalFrame::Navigate() and RemoteFrame::Navigate(), and Gecko’s
nsDocShell::InternalLoad() calls StopLazyLoading(TriggerLoad::No) for
every load but a reload. Stopping at the restore applies that same rule
to the load Ladybird’s restore at creation makes.
